  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「ビャクダン」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%83%93%E3%83%A3%E3%82%AF%E3%83%80%E3%83%B3",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 108912917,
      "text": "ビャクダン はビャクダン科ビャクダン属の半寄生の熱帯性常緑樹。英語名からサンダルウッド とも呼ばれる。爽やかな甘い芳香が特徴。香木として利用される。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Santalum album」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Santalum_album",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1365075216,
      "text": "Santalum album is a small tropical tree, and the traditional source of sandalwood oil. It is native to Indonesia, the Philippines, and Western Australia. It is commonly known as the white sandalwood or Indian sandalwood. It was one of the plants exploited by Austronesian arboriculture and it was introduced by Austronesian sailors to Mainland Southeast Asia, South Asia and East Asia, during the ancient spice trade, becoming naturalized in South India by at least 1300 BCE. It was greatly valued for its fragrance, and is considered sacred in some religions like Hinduism. The high value of the species has caused over-exploitation, to the point where the wild population is vulnerable to extinction. Indian sandalwood still commands high prices for its essential oil owing to its high alpha-santalol content, but the lack of sizable trees has essentially eliminated its former use for fine woodworking. The plant is long-lived, but harvest is only viable after many years."
    }
  },
